Chipalooza Analog Harness for GF GF180MCU
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Chipalooza Analog Harness design was created for the Open Circuit
Design "Chipalooza Challenge", a program to develop analog circuit blocks
for use in SoC designs, make them entirely open source and publicly
available, and make them roughly compatible across the three major open
source PDKs (Sky130, GF180MCU, and GF180MCU).  Measurement and
characterization of the analog blocks requires a test structure, and to
minimize costs, an analog "harness" chip was developed to accommodate
multiple analog blocks with individual and shared resources including
pins, biases, and digital communication.

Efabless, Inc. ran the first Chipalooza challenge in 2024 on the Sky130
process, which resulted in two custom-made test chips that were based on
the Efabless "Caravel" harness.  The Chipalooza harness chip in this
repository is loosely based on the Caravel Openframe (in this case ported
to GF GF180MCU), but differs from the original, partly in pin count (80
padframe pins instead of the original 64, although both designs go in a
64-pin package), and mainly by having specific "slots" for users to place
their analog blocks.  The fixed definition of the slots allows for the
automation of the top-level assembly of the test chip.

The idea behind the "harness" chip is that many circuit designers want to
spend time designing, simulating, testing, validating, and measuring a single
circuit or subsystem for a specific purpose.  Manufacturing and testing that
circuit requires an entire additional layer of skills, work, and time.  The
circuit under test needs significant infrastructure:  Control logic, padframe,
stable voltage and current biases, and an interface to get signals and data
off chip to a host computer and instrumentation.  The purpose of the harness
chip is to provide that infrastructure.

Unlike previous harness designs, the Chipalooza harness is designed such that
each user project has access to dedicated padframe pads, and these pads can
be any pad (from the same foundry I/O set) that the user wishes to have there.
This means that every aspect of the chip is customizable.  Chip top level
assembly requires filling out a configuration file "config.txt" in the top
level directory containing a list of pad types for each project.  Automated
scripts then assemble all of the chip views and layout based on templates
and the configuration file definitions.

This specific project is a three-level hierarchy:

(1) The padframe is a fixed-pin-count 80-pin design which is not based on the
    Efabless "caravel" but is a new design for the Chipalooza test chip
    series.  The size of the chip is fixed according to both the pin count and
    the size and number of spacers, all of which can be modified in the
    padframe template as needed.  

(2) The "chipalooza frame" fills the entire area inside the padframe,
    connecting to power and ground, and analog or general-purpose digital I/O.
    It contains the "housekeeping" SPI, registers, and sequencer, the
    sequencer SRAM, and the project slot power switches, shared current
    and voltage bias generators, and all other shared resources.

(3) A "slot" is an analog user project that fits in one of the eighteen
    avaiable locations in the chipalooza frame wrapper area.

The design flow is described here, with information about how to create an
analog project that fits into a slot, or to create an entirely new Chipalooza
project.  All design steps are handled by scripts.

The Chipalooza repository itself is designed to be as independent of a specific
PDK as possible, so it is relatively easy to port between processes.  Each
process will of course have its own I/O library with its own pad definitions,
which is mainly what drives the process-specific implementation.  While porting
is made convenient, it is not really possible/practical to create one repository
that is compatible with multiple processes.  So it is expected that a Chipalooza
repository is process-specific; this one is for GF GF180MCU.

Unlike the original Efabless implementation, the Chipalooza harness design aims
to have user-defined pad types in each signal pad position, as well as pre-
defined pad types for shared analog and digital resources.  Each foundry PDK has
different analog and digital cells in the I/O library with different inputs and
outputs.  The original openframe harness was for sky130, which had an unusually
complex general-purpose I/O design that was both analog and digital capable.
That made it convenient to design a single fixed padframe supporting both analog
and digital projects.  Since other PDKs don't have the combination I/O, it was
necessary to work out a method by which each pad type can be selected.  But
that implies that the core-facing signals change when any pad changes, so a
large number of verilog, schematic, and layout files have to change accordingly,
which in turn requires a large amount of automation.  This repository provides
that automation.

The process of preparing the Chipalooza harness chip for a tapeout is outlined
below.  The steps require running individual scripts, which have been collected
into a Makefile, where each step is a process of "make <something>", which is
included in the instructions below.

To create a new Chipalooza test chip with custom user analog projects:

(1)  Clone or fork this repository.

	git clone https://github.com/RTimothyEdwards/gf180mcu_ocd_chipalooza
		--recurse-submodules

     Note the use of "--recurse-submodules" to clone the three dependent
     repositories into the top level as git submodules.

     You probably want to create a new branch for the test chip;  then each test
     chip has the same upstream repository but exists in its own development branch.

	git branch tapeout_nov_2026
	git checkout tapeout_nov_2026

(2)  All scripts make use of an environment variable PDK_ROOT which should be set
     appropriately to point to the directory containing the PDK (gf180mcuD),
     e.g.,

	export PDK_ROOT=/foss/pdk

(3)  Edit the "config.txt" file.  This contains a "project_id" value which is an
     arbitrary hex string of 8 digits.  This corresponds to a metal text block on
     the layout, and a digital 32-bit ID value made available to the openframe
     project.  It is recommended that the last four bits of the code be reserved
     for a revision number.

     This is followed by a list of project slot assignments.  For the Open
     Circuit Design Chipalooza challenges, these are all 3rd-party designs and
     are expected to exist in publicly available repositories on github and
     elsewhere.  While there may one day be web-based handling of the slot
     assignments, for now the URL information is entered manually.  Projects
     can then be cloned into submodules:

	make projects

     The slot assignments are followed by a list of repository branches and
     commit hashes.  These will normally be filled out automatically by using
     "make projects", which effectively freezes the state of all submodules.
     After a freeze, if a designer wishes to provide an updated version of their
     design repository, the new commit hash can be entered here and the project
     updated with

	make projects-update

     The slot assignments are followed by a list of signal pads.  The signal pads
     are listed in counter-clockwise order of their physical position in the
     padframe, and the name reflects the associated slot number.  This allows the
     Chipalooza project designers to choose whether each of the signal pads will
     be analog, or digital input, output, or bidirectional, or even a custom pad
     design.  The user does not need to supply any additional information about
     the pads.  The top level pin names will remain the same ("gpio[*]"), and the
     internal signal names will be derived from the pad signal names, with an index.

     The signal pad list entries are in the form:

		sN_an[x]:  pad_name

     where "N" is the project slot number, 1 to 18, and "x" is the index of the
     per-slot pin, where different slots have from 1 to 3 dedicated pins ("x" =
     0 to 2). 

     Example:
		s1_an[0]: gf180mcu_ocd_io__in_cOut4mA

     Core signal names are derived from a signal name.  For the GF180MCU
     technology, if the pad is a digital input-only or digital output-only pad,
     then there is only one core facing signal, which is what is given in the
     config.txt file.  If the pad is an analog pad, there are two signals, one
     of which is the pad name (that is, the core name = the pad name because
     there is a direct connection to the pad and the signals are the same), and
     the other of which is the given signal name.  If the pad is one of the
     "InOut" pads, then there are five core signals <signal_name>_in,
     <signal_name>_out, <signal_name>_ena, <signal_name>_one, and
     <signal_name>_zero.  The first three are the pad-to-core signal, the
     core-to-pad signal, and the output enable signal, respectively.  The
     additional two signals are digital zero and one outputs provided next to
     the pad so that a project can opt to tie off control signals at the pad, or
     to use these to power digital logic close to the pad.  In the example above,
     the core facing signals would be:

		s1_an_0_out
		s1_an_0_in
		s1_an_0_ena
		s1_an_0_one
		s1_an_0_zero

     Note that only the user project dedicated pads can be reassigned.  The power
     pads, SPI pins, and the shared analog and digital pins are fixed.  Note that
     there is nothing technically preventing a user from specifying a ground or
     power pad, but that breaks the intended isolation of each project from the
     global power supplies.  Every specified pad *must* have a wirebond pad,
     or else the wirebond diagram becomes invalid.

     To recap:  For GF180MCU's PDK I/O library, there are eleven pad types, with
     pin and core names derived per the following example:

     1. Input-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_ocd_io__in_c	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signal is:		mysig

     2. Output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_IOPadOut4mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signal is:		mysig

     3. Medium-drive output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_IOPadOut16mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signal is:		mysig

     4. High-drive output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_IOPadOut30mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signal is:		mysig

     5. Tri-state output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_IOPadTriOut4mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig
					mysig_ena

     6. Medium-drive tri-state output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_ocd_io__bi_24t	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig
					mysig_ena
					mysig_zero
					mysig_one

     7. High-drive tri-state output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_IOPadTriOut30mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig
					mysig_ena
					mysig_zero
					mysig_one

     8. Bidrectional pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_ocd_io__in_cOut4mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig_in
					mysig_out
					mysig_ena
					mysig_zero
					mysig_one
     9. Medium-drive bidrectional pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_ocd_io__bi_24t	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig_in
					mysig_out
					mysig_ena
					mysig_zero
					mysig_one

     10. High-drive bidrectional pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_ocd_io__in_cOut30mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig_in
					mysig_out
					mysig_ena
					mysig_zero
					mysig_one

     11. Analog pad:

	gpio[0]:	gf180mcu_ocd_io__in_cOut30mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	gpio[0]
					mysig_esd

	Note that the analog pad is slightly different from other pads:
	For all other pads, the user project wrapper pin names are the
	same as the padframe core-facing pin names.  However, the analog
	pad is connected directly to the core-facing non-ESD pin, so in
	the padframe, the core-facing pin name is the same as the pad name.
	In the user project wrapper, however, the core-facing non-ESD pin
	is given the name "mysig".

     Signals should not be indexed, as then different signals would have
     different index ranges which would not match the indexes of the pads.

     All pads in this library are the same size.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The following steps are for rebuilding the chip top-level with custom
padframe changes due to users who have specified non-default I/O cell
names for the dedicated pads connected to their projects (i.e., in
config.txt one or more "Pad types" have been modified in the previous
step).  If no changes were made to pad types in "config.txt", then
proceed to step (8).
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

(4)  Save the files generated by previous runs with

	make clean-config

     Parse the configuration file with

	make config

     This runs the script "scripts/parse_config.py".  This script reads in
     the configuration file and produces:

	1) A verilog netlist of the padframe in verilog/gl/gf180mcu_padframe.v
	2) A Tcl script used in the next step to generate the padframe and
	   wrapper layouts.
	3) A verilog netlist of the user project wrapper in
	   verilog/gl/chipalooza_frame_wrapper.v.  Note that this module is
	   deprecated and unused, and the module that represents the chip
	   core, which is verilog/rtl/chipalooza_frame.v, is not handled
	   (yet) by the script.

     If the script determines that any of the output files already exist, it
     will not run.  Delete the existing files before re-running the config
     file parser.

     WARNING:  Because the "chipalooza_frame_wrapper" layout and verilog
     contain the user project, MAKE SURE that you do not overwrite existing
     layout and netlist work.  (See comments below about creating the frame
     wrapper layout.)  No verilog netlist is made for the analog user project
     slots, which are assumed to be analog/mixed signal and exist as a schematic,
     not verilog.

(5)  Build the padframe with

	make padframe

     This runs the script "scripts/run_gen_padframe.sh".  This script reads in the
     configuration file and produces:

	1) A padframe layout in magic/gf180mcu_padframe.mag
	2) The user project wrapper layout in magic/openframe_project_wrapper.mag

     This script must be run from the magic/ directory.  If the script determines
     that a file "sg13cmos5_padframe.mag" exists, it will not run.  It may be run
     again at any time by deleting the padframe layout.

(6) Rebuild the project slot wrappers with any custom definitions for
     the pads:

	make wrappers
	make symbols
	make gds-slots

     This runs the script "run_gen_wrapper_gds.sh".
     The script "run_gen_wrapper_gds.sh" creates GDS of the user project
     slot wrappers.  The GDS views of the slot wrappers are what each
     user will use to contain the user project, so these need to be
     defined prior to the users designs, and user specification of
     custom pads must be dealt with first.  However, while a change to an
     individual slot revises all views up to the chip top level, other
     slots are unaffected.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The following steps continue with the top-level chip integration
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

(7)  Set the user ID with

	make user-id

     This step runs the script "set_user_id.py".  This script reads the configuration
     file and produces:

	1) A modified layout "user_id_textblock.mag" with the text of the project
           ID in metal
	2) A modified layout "user_id_vias.mag" with the via programming for the
           32-bit ROM matching the project ID.
	3) A modified "gf180mcu_ocd_chipalooza.v" with the project ID set
	4) A modified "user_id_programming.v" with the project ID bits set

(8) Search the user project repositories for user project GDS:

	make projects-gds

     This runs the script "get_projects_gds.sh" which checks each of
     the user project submodules and finds the "slot_<N>.gds" file,
     including checks for various styles of repository ("final/gds/"
     vs. "gds/"), allowing ".gds.gz" files, and failing if a GDS file
     cannot be found or if both ".gds" and "gds.gz" files exist.

(9) Assemble the chip top level (without fill):

	make gds-prefill

     This step assembles the chip top level prior to fill generation,
     using the script generated in step (12) to drop the user projects
     as "read-only" GDS into the top level layout over top the wrapper
     locations.  It then places the seal ring and writes out the top-
     level GDS. 

(10) Generate fill patterns for the chip using

	make fill

     This step will "cd" to the gds/ directory and run the script
     "generate_fill.py" as follows:

	../scripts/generate_fill.py gf180mcu_ocd_chipalooza.gds.gz -dist 

     This step generates the fill patterns for the chip, in a separate
     GDS file called gf180mcu_ocd_chipalooza_fill_pattern.gds.gz.

(11) Create the final top-level GDS with

	make gds-final

     This step runs the script "run_gen_filled_final_gds.sh" to get the
     (post-fill) final design.  The final top level has its own cell name.
     By default (which is not an GF-approved name) the top level cell will
     be called "gf180mcu_ocd_chipalooza_final".  For GF submissions,
     GF will provide the name of the top level cell as part of the
     submission process.  To get the correct final top level cell name and
     file name, set the environment variable "PROJECT" to the cell name
     before running the script.

(12) Final DRC verification:

	make drc-final

     This step runs the script "run_klayout_drc_final.sh" which runs
     klayout DRC on the final design GDS.  If environment PROJECT has
     been set (see above), then the final DRC result can be found in
     validate/${PROJECT}.lyrdb

(13) Extraction for final LVS verification:

	make extract-sch-top
	make extract-top

     The script "run_extract_chipalooza_top.sh" does a full layout
     extraction on the top level, producing a netlist that is placed
     in netlist/layout/.  If the PROJECT environment variable has been
     set, then the final netlist is netlist/layout/${PROJECT}.spice.

(14) Final LVS:

	make lvs-top

     This runs the script "run_lvs_ocd_chipalooza.sh" which performs
     top-level LVS.

     Note that since the LVS process is highly dependent on what exists
     in the user project area, this script needs to be modified for each
     project.  It is normally set up to start with the top level verilog
     and read the user project from a netlist, which should be extracted
     from the user project schematic.  Everything above the user project
     is in a verilog netlist.  If the top level cell has been named
     according to environment variable PROJECT, then a top level verilog
     netlist needs to be made for a module with the name ${PROJECT}.  This
     is easy to do by copying the "gf180mcu_ocd_chipalooza_final.v"
     file to ${PROJECT}.v and changing the module name to ${PROJECT}.

     [To do:  Currently top level LVS through magic and netgen fails due
     to a single issue with failure to cleanly separate the 5.0V and 3.3V
     ground domains.  In addition, some cell views are problematic and
     dealt with by treating them as "black boxes", such as the GF SRAM,
     and these problems should be investigated and corrected.]

     Checking LVS is done with magic and netgen.  LVS with klayout has
     not been attempted and would require converting the verilog of the
     padframe, wrapper, and top level cell to SPICE/CDL.
     
(15) (Item to do, not yet automated)
     Preparation for foundry submission:

     GF has its own requirements for chip submission that are not
     exactly compatible with this repository;  the entire project needs
     to be copied into a compatible repository with various directories
     populated as dictated by GF.  This step will be automated.
